  },
  "verdictDnsProblemTitle": "DNS problem",
  "@verdictDnsProblemTitle": {
    "description": "Diagnosis verdict headline: names do not resolve but IPs are reachable."
  },
  "verdictDnsProblemDetail": "The Internet is reachable, but website names are not being translated into addresses. This is a DNS issue and is usually easy to fix by switching to a public DNS resolver.",
  "@verdictDnsProblemDetail": {
    "description": "Diagnosis verdict body for a DNS resolution failure."
  },
  "solutionDnsProblemMessageMobile": "1. Switch your Private DNS to a reliable public resolver such as 1.1.1.1 (Cloudflare) or 8.8.8.8 (Google), then test again.\n2. If it still fails, toggle mobile data off and on, or restart your phone, to get a fresh connection.\n3. If it still fails, contact your mobile carrier — some carriers run DNS resolvers that have outages of their own.",
  "@solutionDnsProblemMessageMobile": {
    "description": "What-to-do text for a DNS problem on a cellular link."
  },
  "solutionDnsProblemMessageFixed": "1. Switch your Private DNS to a reliable public resolver such as 1.1.1.1 (Cloudflare) or 8.8.8.8 (Google), then test again.\n2. If you are on a managed network (work, school, public Wi-Fi), contact the network administrator to fix the DNS.\n3. If you are on your own network, check your router settings. It is good practice to configure the secondary DNS to a public resolver as well (examples: 1.1.1.1, 4.4.4.4, 4.4.2.2, 8.8.8.8), in case the primary (your Internet provider) fails.",
  "@solutionDnsProblemMessageFixed": {
    "description": "What-to-do text for a DNS problem on Wi-Fi/wired."
  },
